WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.048 --> 00:00:03.969
The air is still. There is a creaking sound.

2
00:00:03.970 --> 00:00:09.636
The lid of the ancient coffin slowly opens. Everyone holds their breath.

3
00:00:09.637 --> 00:00:16.353
I gasp, then breathe a sigh of reliefㅡI see nothing but darkness inside the coffin.

4
00:00:16.354 --> 00:00:22.709
But the coffin is not empty. Someoneㅡor somethingㅡis in the coffin.

5
00:00:22.710 --> 00:00:33.100
It is a mummy. It rises with a jerky movement, and I gasp again. Its face and its entire body are covered loosely in bandages.

6
00:00:33.101 --> 00:00:40.933
The ragged bandages are yellow with age. We are frightened, but our eyes are fixed on this incredible sight.

7
00:00:40.934 --> 00:00:45.019
The mummy sits up completely and slowly turns to us.

8
00:00:45.020 --> 00:00:49.257
It begins to mutter ancient prayers that echo through the chamber.

9
00:00:49.258 --> 00:00:53.010
"What’s it saying?" I ask a fellow archeologist.

10
00:00:53.011 --> 00:01:01.583
"We must stay with him," my colleague answers, in a strange monotone. "We must stay with him . . . forever."

11
00:01:01.584 --> 00:01:03.662
Did that scene sound familiar?

12
00:01:03.663 --> 00:01:14.763
It could have come from any number of horror movies: The Mummy, The Mummy’s Revenge, The Mummy’s Ghost, The Mummy’s Hand, or The Mummy’s Tomb

13
00:01:14.764 --> 00:01:21.245
. The mummy is a fixture in Hollywood movies, but what is the real story of the

14
00:01:21.246 --> 00:01:28.158
mummy? Where do mummies come from? How are they made? Is there really a mummy’s

15
00:01:28.159 --> 00:01:30.968
curse? A mummy is a preserved

16
00:01:30.969 --> 00:01:39.801
corpse. Most mummies were made deliberately, but some mummies have been preserved accidentally due to environmental

17
00:01:39.802 --> 00:01:44.134
conditions. The earliest mummy dates back to 3300

18
00:01:44.135 --> 00:01:49.204
BC. It is a man with red hair and is displayed in the British

19
00:01:49.205 --> 00:01:52.878
Museum. He was buried in the hot desert sand in

20
00:01:52.879 --> 00:01:58.958
Africa. The hot, dry weather dried the body and prevented the flesh from

21
00:01:58.959 --> 00:02:08.937
decaying. By 3030 BC, it was common practice in ancient Egypt to mummify the dead bodies of kings, queens, and the

22
00:02:08.938 --> 00:02:15.698
nobility. This practice continued for over three thousand years and produced over seventy million

23
00:02:15.699 --> 00:02:20.694
mummies. Ancient Egypt is not the only civilization to preserve its

24
00:02:20.695 --> 00:02:25.916
dead. Ancient cultures in Peru and Siberia also practiced

25
00:02:25.917 --> 00:02:32.094
mummification. People in these cultures preserved the bodies of the dead because they believed in life after

26
00:02:32.095 --> 00:02:37.761
death. They believed that a person’s body must retain its physical appearance after

27
00:02:37.762 --> 00:02:42.586
death. The best way to do this was to preserve the body by

28
00:02:42.587 --> 00:02:53.741
mummification. In ancient Egypt, the mummification process took seventy days and was done by special priests who performed prayers at different stages in the

29
00:02:53.742 --> 00:02:58.314
process. These priests needed a detailed knowledge of human

30
00:02:58.315 --> 00:03:04.735
anatomy. The first step in mummification was removing all parts of the body that would decay

31
00:03:04.736 --> 00:03:10.977
quickly. First, a hook was stuck up the deceased person’s nose and the brain was pulled

32
00:03:10.978 --> 00:03:16.303
out. This was a delicate procedure because the face could easily be

33
00:03:16.304 --> 00:03:24.214
disfigured. The priest then removed the internal organs, leaving only the heart, which was believed to be the center of

34
00:03:24.215 --> 00:03:26.722
intelligence. The organs were preserved

35
00:03:26.723 --> 00:03:37.533
separately. The stomach, liver, lungs, and intestines were placed in special jars called canopic jars, which were later buried with the

36
00:03:37.534 --> 00:03:44.108
mummy. Next, the priests used a kind of salt called natron to dry out the

37
00:03:44.109 --> 00:03:50.616
body. All moisture was removed and the result was a very dried-out but recognizable human

38
00:03:50.617 --> 00:03:57.859
form. The next part in the process was to fill the sunken areas of the body with linen and add false

39
00:03:57.860 --> 00:04:06.263
eyes. Then, the body was covered in oil, spices, and perfume and wrapped in linen bandages coated with

40
00:04:06.264 --> 00:04:09.430
resin. Each mummy needed hundreds of yards of

41
00:04:09.431 --> 00:04:21.324
linen. The priests carefully wound long strips of linen around the body, sometimes even wrapping each finger and toe separately before wrapping the entire hand or

42
00:04:21.325 --> 00:04:27.915
foot. Charms were placed among the linen strips, and prayers were written on some strips for

43
00:04:27.916 --> 00:04:34.579
protection. At several stages, the body was coated with warm resin, and the wrapping resumed once

44
00:04:34.580 --> 00:04:41.568
again. When the wrapping was finished, the priests covered the body with a shroud. The mummy was complete.

45
00:04:41.569 --> 00:04:46.968
People have been interested in mummies for thousands of years, although their reasons have varied.

46
00:04:46.969 --> 00:04:56.896
Soon after the pharaohs were mummified and buried, thieves became very interested in their mummies because they were buried in tombs filled with golden treasures.

47
00:04:56.897 --> 00:05:05.722
Tomb robbing became so common that the mummies were moved to secret burial places, where some remained undisturbed for thousands of years.

48
00:05:05.723 --> 00:05:16.694
In the Middle Ages, it was believed that certain body parts of the mummies had medicinal properties, and so they were ground up into "mummy powder" for use in potions.

49
00:05:16.695 --> 00:05:25.029
In 1798, Napoleon invaded Egypt and took scientists with him to record Egyptian culture and religion.

50
00:05:25.030 --> 00:05:32.528
The research of these scientists was later published in Europe and included their investigation of Egyptian mummies.

51
00:05:32.529 --> 00:05:39.282
"Egyptology," or the study of ancient Egypt, became very popular among the upper classes.

52
00:05:39.283 --> 00:05:47.608
European aristocrats became fond of collecting mummies; they would even hold special parties at which a mummy would be unwrapped.

53
00:05:47.609 --> 00:05:52.343
Many nobles funded or led archeological expeditions to Egypt.

54
00:05:52.344 --> 00:05:58.175
However, most of the ancient tombs they explored had already been disturbed or robbed.

55
00:05:58.176 --> 00:06:06.998
In 1891, a young Englishman named Howard Carter arrived in Egypt and trained for many years as an archeologist.

56
00:06:06.999 --> 00:06:11.649
Carter was convinced that there was at least one undiscovered tomb.

57
00:06:11.650 --> 00:06:18.663
Under the patronage of Lord Carnarvon, a wealthy English aristocrat, Carter searched for tombs.

58
00:06:18.664 --> 00:06:31.726
In 1922, Lord Carnarvon summoned him to England to tell him the funding for his research had run out, but Carter convinced Lord Carnarvon to fund one final expedition.

59
00:06:31.727 --> 00:06:38.892
On November 4, 1922, Carter’s team discovered an ancient doorway that was still sealed.

60
00:06:38.893 --> 00:06:41.968
On the doorway was the name "Tutankhamun".

61
00:06:41.969 --> 00:06:48.546
Upon discovering the untouched tomb, Howard Carter told Lord Carnarvon to come to Egypt immediately.

62
00:06:48.547 --> 00:06:52.622
When the tomb door was opened, there were many celebrations.

63
00:06:52.623 --> 00:07:02.956
They found the tomb undisturbed, and inside were many treasures, including the mummy of Pharaoh Tutankhamun and three gold coffins nested inside each other.

64
00:07:02.957 --> 00:07:09.616
There were rumors that Carter also found a tablet inscribed with a curse but immediately hid it from his workers.

65
00:07:09.617 --> 00:07:17.353
A number of mysterious deaths followed, and it was thought that Tutankhamun’s mummy had cursed those who had disturbed his tomb.

66
00:07:17.354 --> 00:07:21.532
First, Lord Carnarvon died in Cairo of pneumonia.

67
00:07:21.533 --> 00:07:23.683
This created a media frenzy.

68
00:07:23.684 --> 00:07:27.272
News of the mummy’s curse circulated around the world.

69
00:07:27.273 --> 00:07:41.343
Legend has it, that by 1929, eleven of the people connected with the discovery of the tomb had died, including two of Lord Carnarvon’s relatives, and Carter’s personal secretary, Richard Bethell.

70
00:07:41.344 --> 00:07:51.408
The bodies of the dead pharaohs, queens, and nobility of ancient Egypt weren’t the only things to be mummifiedㅡthe ancient Egyptians also mummified animals!

71
00:07:51.409 --> 00:07:57.234
Some cults in ancient Egypt worshipped animals, like the cat goddess Bast.

72
00:07:57.235 --> 00:08:04.078
Thousands of cats were mummified for sale to pilgrims, who presented them as offerings to the cat goddess.

73
00:08:04.079 --> 00:08:08.827
Vast cemeteries have been discovered containing thousands of buried cats.

74
00:08:08.828 --> 00:08:14.389
It is likely that cats were bred especially to be sacrificed to the Egyptian cat goddess.

75
00:08:14.390 --> 00:08:26.972
The Egyptians believed that their gods could become certain animalsㅡcats, hawks, crocodiles, and bullsㅡand these animals were protected, worshipped, and mummified at death.

76
00:08:26.973 --> 00:08:33.979
Much of our knowledge of Egyptian mummification comes from one historian in particular: Herodotus.

77
00:08:33.980 --> 00:08:43.147
Herodotus was an ancient Greek who observed and recorded how bodies were mummified on his travels through Egypt in 450 BC.

78
00:08:43.148 --> 00:08:46.889
Mummification was practiced for over three thousand years.

79
00:08:46.890 --> 00:08:51.307
In 30 BC, Egypt became a province of the Roman Empire.

80
00:08:51.308 --> 00:09:01.461
The Roman presence in ancient Egypt eventually led to the decline of the practice of mummification and the replacement of ancient religions with Christianity.

81
00:09:01.462 --> 00:09:08.372
Nowadays, the technology of x-rays and computers allows us to examine mummies without unwrapping them.

82
00:09:08.373 --> 00:09:10.872
Their bones can be scanned and studied.

83
00:09:10.873 --> 00:09:14.263
DNA samples can be extracted and analyzed.

84
00:09:14.264 --> 00:09:19.026
Although dead for thousands of years, the mummies continue to speak to us.

